; pi4DmaGate.pi4 - THE DIFFERENTIAL GATE for RaspberryPi4/Lib/dma.pi4.
;
; Do the same rectangle twice - once with a plain CPU byte loop and once
; with the DMA engine - and require the two results to be IDENTICAL BYTE
; FOR BYTE, over the WHOLE buffer and not merely over the rectangle.
;
; DIAGNOSTIC. Does not ship. It touches no framebuffer, opens no
; mailbox, and needs no HDMI display connected.
;
;   PureMetalForge.exe --compile RaspberryPi4\Examples\Diagnostics\pi4DmaGate.pi4 -t pi4 \
;        --load-addr 0x400000 --stack-addr 0x3000000 --entry-returns \
;        -o _work\dmagate.img
;
; ======================================================================
; WHY A CPU BYTE LOOP IS THE RIGHT ORACLE
; ======================================================================
; pi4FpGate.pi4 makes the argument for this shape of test and it applies
; here unchanged: a table of expected answers can be wrong in the same
; direction as the code it checks, and two INDEPENDENT IMPLEMENTATIONS
; of the same specification, running on the same board, in the same
; program, on the same inputs, cannot.
;
; The oracle here is fifteen lines of PokeB. It shares nothing with
; dma.pi4 - not a register, not a control block, not a cache operation,
; not even a word width. If the two agree on a hundred geometries the
; agreement means something.
;
; AND THE COMPARISON IS OVER THE WHOLE 64 KiB, NOT THE RECTANGLE. That
; is the half that catches the interesting bugs. A stride applied
; instead of added, a YLENGTH one too large, a burst that over-writes
; past the end of a row - none of those corrupt the rectangle. They
; corrupt what is NEXT to it, and a test that only looked at the
; rectangle would pass every one of them.
;
; ======================================================================
; WHAT IS SWEPT, AND WHAT EACH GROUP IS LOOKING FOR
; ======================================================================
;
;   GROUP A - FILL, ALIGNED. Row lengths that are multiples of four
;     bytes, at pitches with and without a gap, from one row to
;     seventeen. This is the ordinary case and every one of these must
;     pass.
;
;   GROUP B - FILL, AWKWARD. Row lengths that are NOT multiples of four
;     - 5, 17, 63 bytes - and destinations that are not four-byte
;     aligned. The PDF says the engine "can deal with byte aligned
;     transfers and will minimise bus traffic by buffering and packing
;     misaligned accesses" (bcm2711-peripherals.txt:2286), so these
;     should pass; they are in their own group because if they DO fail,
;     the finding is "align your rectangles", not "the library is
;     broken", and the two must not be confused.
;
;   GROUP C - COPY. The same geometries with a real source, and the
;     source deliberately has a DIFFERENT pitch from the destination on
;     half of them, because one stride field serving both sides is
;     exactly where a packing bug would hide.
;
;   GROUP D - THE OVERLAPPING DOWNWARD COPY. dma.pi4 permits it on the
;     reasoning that both addresses increment, which the PDF states,
;     and therefore that each byte is read before the write that would
;     clobber it, which the PDF does NOT state. This group is the whole
;     evidence for that assumption. If it fails, the fix is in
;     dma.pi4's DmaCopy2D - refuse overlap in both directions - and not
;     here.
;
;   GROUP E - THE REFUSALS. Rectangles that must be turned away before
;     a register is written. These matter more than they look: this
;     engine writes physical addresses with nothing in the path to stop
;     it, and a guard nobody has watched refuse anything is a guard
;     nobody knows is broken.
;
;   GROUP A2 - THE BRACKET. Added after the first bench run, which
;     failed exactly one case and left the boundary unlocated. Two
;     ladders that walk the row length and the destination stride
;     SEPARATELY, so a failure names which of the two it was.
;
;   GROUP G - CACHE COHERENCY, AND THE ONLY GROUP WITH NEGATIVE
;     CONTROLS. Added 2026-08-27 when display.pi4 gained a DMA path.
;     Groups A to F prove GEOMETRY and prove nothing whatever about
;     coherency, because DgCaseFill and DgCaseCopy call DmaCacheRange
;     over the whole 64 KiB on both sides of every transfer - which
;     satisfies the discipline for them and would keep them green with
;     every cache operation in dma.pi4 deleted. Group G removes that
;     hygiene deliberately, one operation at a time, and requires the
;     result to come out WRONG. Its own header is the long version.
;     It runs BEFORE group F, because a group behind a deliberate hang
;     may never run.
;
;   GROUP F - THE BURST CEILING, AND IT IS EXPECTED TO HANG THE BOARD.
;     It runs last, it is the only caller of DmaSetBurstProbe(), and
;     ARMING THE DEADMAN IS NOT OPTIONAL BEFORE IT. Its own header has
;     the bisection procedure that turns the search into three runs.
;
; ======================================================================
; ARM THE DEADMAN. `deadman 15` at the Anvil prompt, then `run`.
; ======================================================================
; Group F provoked an AXI bus lock on 2026-08-26 and the deadman is
; what brought the board back. The counter is 20 bits at 65536 Hz, so
; ~15.999 s is the ceiling on this part (safety.pi4:491-498 clamps and
; RETURNS the real value); the whole suite runs in a couple of seconds,
; so 15 covers it with room to spare and every group F rung is
; deliberately one case rather than three so it stays that way.
;
; ======================================================================
; THE BUFFERS, AND THE PROPERTY THE LAYOUT ITSELF PROVES
; ======================================================================
;   $780000  REF, 64 KiB - what the CPU produced
;   $790000  OUT, 64 KiB - what the engine produced
;   $7A0000  SRC, 64 KiB - the source for the copy tests
;   $800000  THRASH, 2 MiB - group G's eviction region. Never read for
;            its contents, only walked so that the last-level cache
;            cannot still be holding the three buffers above.
;
; DmaSetBounds() IS POINTED AT OUT AND NOTHING ELSE, for the whole run.
; So REF and SRC are outside the window the library will write into,
; and any transfer that aimed at them - through a sign error, an
; overflowed stride, a rectangle computed backwards - is refused rather
; than delivered. The layout is part of the test.
;
; All three are inside the low gigabyte, which is the one address rule
; the legacy engines have and the one that fails silently. See dma.pi4's
; HAZARD 1.
;
; ======================================================================
; THE CACHES
; ======================================================================
; #DG_MMU = 1 runs the whole gate with DRAM Normal Write-Back Cacheable,
; which is the configuration that actually matters now and the one where
; a missing clean or a missing invalidate turns a correct transfer into
; a wrong answer. Every case here cleans what the CPU wrote before the
; engine reads it and invalidates what the engine wrote before the CPU
; reads it, through DmaCacheRange().
;
; RUNNING IT WITH #DG_MMU = 0 AS WELL IS WORTH ONE RUN. With the caches
; off the maintenance calls are architecturally no-ops, so a pass there
; and a failure here would localise the fault to the cache discipline
; rather than to the transfer - which is the one diagnosis that is hard
; to reach any other way.
;
; BUT SEE GROUP G FIRST. The sentence above ("every case here cleans
; what the CPU wrote... through DmaCacheRange") is exactly why groups A
; to F cannot certify the cache discipline: the GATE does the
; maintenance, generously, on both sides of every transfer, so the
; library could do none of its own and every case would still pass.
; Group G is the part that removes the gate's own hygiene on purpose and
; requires the result to be wrong. With #DG_MMU = 0 group G announces
; that it is inert and runs no cases, because with the caches off there
; is no hazard to provoke and a green group G would mean nothing.

XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/uart.pi4"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/mmu.pi4"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/dma.pi4"


#DG_MMU = 1

#DG_TABLES = $600000
#DG_SCRATCH = $700000
#DG_SCRATCH_BYTES = 512

#DG_REF = $780000
#DG_OUT = $790000
#DG_SRC = $7A0000
#DG_BYTES = 65536

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE THRASH REGION - two megabytes whose only job is to be bigger than
;  the cache. Added 2026-08-27 with group G.
;
;  Group G has to FORCE an eviction, because the hazard it looks for is a
;  dirty line written back AFTER the engine has already written the same
;  address. Waiting for that to happen by itself is not a test; the whole
;  difficulty of the bug is that it happens when it feels like it.
;  Reading straight through a region larger than the last level of cache
;  makes it happen on demand.
;
;  TWO MEGABYTES because the Cortex-A72 cluster in the BCM2711 has a 1 MB
;  shared L2 above a 32 KiB L1 data cache. Two megabytes read end to end
;  cannot leave any of the 192 KiB of gate buffers resident, whatever the
;  replacement policy does.
;
;  $800000 is above the three buffers, below the stack top at $3000000,
;  and inside the low payload window that THE MEMORY MAP in
;  RaspberryPi4/Board/memmap.pi4 sets aside - up to $07EFFFFF; its base
;  is above the monitor and `map` prints it. Nothing reads what it
;  contains - only that it was touched.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#DG_THRASH = $800000
#DG_THRASH_BYTES = 2097152

; ASCII codes. No character literals - house rule.
; ======================================================================
;  TRACING - because silence means pass, and a hang is not a pass
; ======================================================================
;  Every group except F prints only failures, which keeps a clean run
;  to a handful of lines. That convention is correct for a test that
;  RETURNS and useless for one that might not: on 2026-08-26 group F
;  hung the board and the transcript could not say which rung it was
;  on, because a rung that hangs never reaches its own failure line.
;
;  A HANG IS ONLY EVER DIAGNOSED BY WHAT WAS PRINTED BEFORE IT.
;
;  So there are two levels, and the split is on cost:
;
;    #DG_TRACE = 0  the default. Groups A to E stay silent on success.
;                   They run inside DmaSetBurst()'s cap and none of
;                   them has ever hung.
;    #DG_TRACE = 1  every case in every group announces its geometry
;                   BEFORE it attempts it, and drains the UART so the
;                   bytes are physically on the wire before the engine
;                   starts. Sixty-odd extra lines, and the last one is
;                   the answer if the board stops.
;
;  IF THE BOARD EVER HANGS IN A GROUP OTHER THAN F, re-run once with
;  #DG_TRACE = 1. That is the whole recovery procedure.
;
;  GROUP F IS ALWAYS TRACED REGARDLESS, because provoking a hang is its
;  entire purpose and a traceless run of it is a wasted reset.
#DG_TRACE = 0

#DG_ZERO  = 48
#DG_ALPHA = 55                 ; the letter A, minus ten
#DG_SPACE = 32

Global dgChecks.i = 0
Global dgFails.i = 0

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  A THIRD TALLY, AND IT IS NOT A THIRD KIND OF FAILURE.
;
;  Group G carries NEGATIVE CONTROLS: cases that deliberately omit a
;  cache operation and must therefore come out WRONG. A negative control
;  that comes out RIGHT has not found a bug in dma.pi4 - it has found
;  that the gate could not provoke the hazard on this run, which means
;  the matching POSITIVE case proved nothing either.
;
;  Calling that a pass would be the worst possible answer, because it is
;  the answer a machine with the caches accidentally off gives to every
;  question in the group. Calling it a failure would be a lie about
;  dma.pi4. So it is its own word: INCONCLUSIVE, counted, and printed in
;  the summary next to the other two.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Global dgIncon.i = 0


; ======================================================================
;  OUTPUT
; ======================================================================

Procedure PutS(*s)
  UartWriteStr(*s)
EndProcedure

Procedure PutNl()
  PrintNl()
EndProcedure

Procedure PutHexN(v.i, digits.i)
  Define i.i
  Define n.i
  For i = digits - 1 To 0 Step -1
    n = (v >> (i * 4)) & $F
    If n < 10
      UartWrite(#DG_ZERO + n)
    Else
      UartWrite(#DG_ALPHA + n)
    EndIf
  Next
EndProcedure

; Print a geometry the same way every time, so a failing line can be
; read at a glance and typed straight back in.
Procedure PutGeom(rowBytes.i, rows.i, dpitch.i, spitch.i, off.i)
  PutS("w=")
  PrintDec(rowBytes)
  PutS(" h=")
  PrintDec(rows)
  PutS(" dp=")
  PrintDec(dpitch)
  PutS(" sp=")
  PrintDec(spitch)
  PutS(" off=")
  PrintDec(off)
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  THE ORACLE - a plain CPU byte loop, sharing nothing with dma.pi4
; ======================================================================

; Every byte of a buffer set to one value. PokeB, one byte at a time,
; because the oracle must not depend on any width assumption that the
; thing under test also makes.
Procedure DgSet(addr.i, bytes.i, v.i)
  Define i.i
  i = 0
  While i < bytes
    PokeB(addr + i, v & $FF)
    i = i + 1
  Wend
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; DgFillRef - the fill, as the CPU would do it.
;
; Byte j of a row gets byte (j mod 4) of the pattern word, and that is
; the specification the engine is being held to. It is worth saying why
; it is right even though the engine reads the pattern 128 bits at a
; time from a source address that never moves: every one of those reads
; starts at the pattern base, 16 is a multiple of 4, and the pattern is
; one 32-bit word repeated - so whatever chunk size the engine chooses,
; the byte it delivers at row offset j is pattern byte (j mod 4). The
; oracle is therefore independent of the engine's chunking, which is
; exactly what an oracle has to be.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure DgFillRef(dst.i, pitch.i, rowBytes.i, rows.i, pattern.i)
  Define r.i
  Define j.i
  Define b.i
  r = 0
  While r < rows
    j = 0
    While j < rowBytes
      b = (pattern >> ((j & 3) * 8)) & $FF
      PokeB((dst + (r * pitch)) + j, b)
      j = j + 1
    Wend
    r = r + 1
  Wend
EndProcedure

; The copy, as the CPU would do it. Forward, byte at a time, which is
; also the order dma.pi4 claims the engine uses - so for the
; overlapping case in group D this oracle is asserting the SAME
; ordering, and the two agreeing is the evidence.
Procedure DgCopyRef(dst.i, dpitch.i, src.i, spitch.i, rowBytes.i, rows.i)
  Define r.i
  Define j.i
  r = 0
  While r < rows
    j = 0
    While j < rowBytes
      PokeB((dst + (r * dpitch)) + j, PeekB((src + (r * spitch)) + j) & $FF)
      j = j + 1
    Wend
    r = r + 1
  Wend
EndProcedure

; First differing byte offset, or -1 if the two buffers are identical.
; The OFFSET is the diagnosis - it says whether the damage was inside
; the rectangle, in the gap after a row, or past the end of the last
; row, and those are three different bugs.
Procedure.i DgDiff(a.i, b.i, bytes.i)
  Define i.i
  i = 0
  While i < bytes
    If (PeekB(a + i) & $FF) <> (PeekB(b + i) & $FF)
      ProcedureReturn i
    EndIf
    i = i + 1
  Wend
  ProcedureReturn -1
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; DgReport - one case, reported the same way whatever failed.
;
; A failing line carries the geometry, the byte offset, and both bytes,
; because those four facts together are usually the whole diagnosis and
; a bench operator should not have to instrument anything to get them.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure DgReport(*group, rowBytes.i, rows.i, dpitch.i, spitch.i, off.i, diff.i)
  dgChecks = dgChecks + 1
  If diff < 0
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  dgFails = dgFails + 1
  PutS("  FAIL  ")
  PutS(*group)
  PutS("  ")
  PutGeom(rowBytes, rows, dpitch, spitch, off)
  PutS("   first bad byte at +")
  PrintDec(diff)
  PutS(" ($")
  PutHexN(diff, 4)
  PutS("):  cpu $")
  PutHexN(PeekB(#DG_REF + diff) & $FF, 2)
  PutS("  dma $")
  PutHexN(PeekB(#DG_OUT + diff) & $FF, 2)

  ; Say WHERE it is relative to the rectangle, because that is the part
  ; a human would otherwise work out on paper every single time.
  If diff >= (((rows - 1) * dpitch) + rowBytes + off)
    PutS("   PAST THE END of the last row")
  Else
    If (((diff - off) % dpitch) >= rowBytes)
      PutS("   in the GAP after a row - the stride is wrong")
    Else
      PutS("   INSIDE the rectangle")
    EndIf
  EndIf
  PutNl()
EndProcedure

; A refusal that must happen. Same tally, different shape.
Procedure DgMustRefuse(*name, got.i, wantErr.i)
  dgChecks = dgChecks + 1
  If got <> 0
    dgFails = dgFails + 1
    PutS("  FAIL  ")
    PutS(*name)
    PutS("   the transfer was ACCEPTED and it must not be")
    PutNl()
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  If DmaError() <> wantErr
    dgFails = dgFails + 1
    PutS("  FAIL  ")
    PutS(*name)
    PutS("   refused, but for the wrong reason: ")
    PutS(DmaErrorText())
    PutNl()
  EndIf
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  ONE CASE
; ======================================================================

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; DgCaseFill - one fill geometry, both ways, whole-buffer comparison.
;
; THE TWO BUFFERS ARE PRE-SET TO THE SAME NON-ZERO BYTE, not to zero.
; Zero is the value a failed transfer leaves behind and the value fresh
; memory tends to hold, so a test that filled zeros into zeros could
; pass while doing nothing at all. $3C is arbitrary and is neither the
; pattern nor a byte of it.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; DgAnnounce - name a case BEFORE attempting it, and get the bytes out.
;
; THE UartDrain() IS THE ENTIRE POINT and it is not tidiness. Without
; it the line sits in the PL011's transmit FIFO while the DMA engine
; starts, and if the engine locks the bus the CPU never runs the code
; that would have pushed it out - so the marker that was supposed to
; name the rung dies in the FIFO with everything else. Drain first,
; then touch the hardware.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure DgAnnounce(*what, rowBytes.i, rows.i, dpitch.i, spitch.i, off.i)
  UartWriteStr("  .. ")
  UartWriteStr(*what)
  UartWriteStr("  ")
  PutGeom(rowBytes, rows, dpitch, spitch, off)
  PutNl()
  UartDrain()
EndProcedure

Procedure DgCaseFill(rowBytes.i, rows.i, pitch.i, off.i, pattern.i)
  Define rc.i

  If #DG_TRACE = 1
    DgAnnounce("fill", rowBytes, rows, pitch, 0, off)
  EndIf

  DgSet(#DG_REF, #DG_BYTES, $3C)
  DgSet(#DG_OUT, #DG_BYTES, $3C)
  DmaCacheRange(#DG_OUT, #DG_BYTES)

  DgFillRef(#DG_REF + off, pitch, rowBytes, rows, pattern)

  rc = DmaFillPattern32(#DG_OUT + off, pitch, rowBytes, rows, pattern)
  If rc = 0
    dgChecks = dgChecks + 1
    dgFails  = dgFails + 1
    PutS("  FAIL  A/B fill  ")
    PutGeom(rowBytes, rows, pitch, 0, off)
    PutS("   REFUSED: ")
    PutS(DmaErrorText())
    PutNl()
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf

  DmaCacheRange(#DG_OUT, #DG_BYTES)
  DgReport("fill", rowBytes, rows, pitch, 0, off, DgDiff(#DG_REF, #DG_OUT, #DG_BYTES))
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; DgCaseCopy - one copy geometry, both ways, whole-buffer comparison.
;
; The source is filled with a counter rather than a constant, so a copy
; that read the same row repeatedly, or that walked the source with the
; destination's stride, produces a different answer instead of the same
; one. A constant source would pass both of those bugs.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure DgCaseCopy(rowBytes.i, rows.i, dpitch.i, spitch.i, off.i)
  Define rc.i
  Define i.i

  If #DG_TRACE = 1
    DgAnnounce("copy", rowBytes, rows, dpitch, spitch, off)
  EndIf

  DgSet(#DG_REF, #DG_BYTES, $3C)
  DgSet(#DG_OUT, #DG_BYTES, $3C)
  i = 0
  While i < #DG_BYTES
    PokeB(#DG_SRC + i, (i + (i / 251)) & $FF)
    i = i + 1
  Wend
  DmaCacheRange(#DG_SRC, #DG_BYTES)
  DmaCacheRange(#DG_OUT, #DG_BYTES)

  DgCopyRef(#DG_REF + off, dpitch, #DG_SRC, spitch, rowBytes, rows)

  rc = DmaCopy2D(#DG_OUT + off, dpitch, #DG_SRC, spitch, rowBytes, rows)
  If rc = 0
    dgChecks = dgChecks + 1
    dgFails  = dgFails + 1
    PutS("  FAIL  A/B copy  ")
    PutGeom(rowBytes, rows, dpitch, spitch, off)
    PutS("   REFUSED: ")
    PutS(DmaErrorText())
    PutNl()
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf

  DmaCacheRange(#DG_OUT, #DG_BYTES)
  DgReport("copy", rowBytes, rows, dpitch, spitch, off, DgDiff(#DG_REF, #DG_OUT, #DG_BYTES))
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; DgCaseScroll - an OVERLAPPING downward copy, both ways.
;
; This is the one case in the gate that tests an assumption rather than
; a documented fact, and it is the reason dma.pi4 allows overlap at all
; instead of forcing every scroll through a temporary buffer and a
; second pass over the bytes.
;
; Both buffers are seeded identically first, so the CPU reference and
; the engine are shifting the SAME starting contents. The reference
; copies forward, byte at a time, which is the order the engine is
; claimed to use; the two agreeing is the evidence for the claim.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure DgCaseScroll(rowBytes.i, rows.i, pitch.i, shift.i)
  Define rc.i
  Define i.i
  Define n.i

  If #DG_TRACE = 1
    DgAnnounce("scroll", rowBytes, rows, pitch, pitch, shift)
  EndIf

  n = ((rows + shift) * pitch)

  DgSet(#DG_REF, #DG_BYTES, $3C)
  DgSet(#DG_OUT, #DG_BYTES, $3C)
  i = 0
  While i < n
    PokeB(#DG_REF + i, (i * 7) & $FF)
    PokeB(#DG_OUT + i, (i * 7) & $FF)
    i = i + 1
  Wend
  DmaCacheRange(#DG_OUT, #DG_BYTES)

  DgCopyRef(#DG_REF, pitch, #DG_REF + (shift * pitch), pitch, rowBytes, rows)

  rc = DmaCopy2D(#DG_OUT, pitch, #DG_OUT + (shift * pitch), pitch, rowBytes, rows)
  If rc = 0
    dgChecks = dgChecks + 1
    dgFails  = dgFails + 1
    PutS("  FAIL  A/B scroll  ")
    PutGeom(rowBytes, rows, pitch, pitch, shift)
    PutS("   REFUSED: ")
    PutS(DmaErrorText())
    PutNl()
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf

  DmaCacheRange(#DG_OUT, #DG_BYTES)
  DgReport("scroll", rowBytes, rows, pitch, pitch, shift, DgDiff(#DG_REF, #DG_OUT, #DG_BYTES))
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  THE SWEEPS
; ======================================================================

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; GROUP A - fill, aligned. The ordinary case, and every one must pass.
;
; The geometries are chosen to cross the boundaries the hardware cares
; about rather than to be numerous: 4 bytes is one 32-bit write and
; smaller than the engine's 128-bit width, 16 is exactly that width, 20
; crosses it awkwardly, 64 is a cache line, 68 crosses one, 252 and 256
; straddle the point where a burst can and cannot complete. One row
; tests the degenerate case where the stride is never applied at all,
; and seventeen rows tests it sixteen times.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure DgGroupA()
  PutS("-- group A: fill, four-byte-aligned rows and destinations")
  PutNl()

  DgCaseFill(4,    1,   4,   0, $11223344)
  DgCaseFill(4,    1,  64,   0, $11223344)
  DgCaseFill(4,    8,  64,   0, $11223344)
  DgCaseFill(16,   1,  16,   0, $A5A5A5A5)
  DgCaseFill(16,   4,  16,   0, $A5A5A5A5)
  DgCaseFill(16,   4,  64,   0, $A5A5A5A5)
  DgCaseFill(20,   3,  64,   0, $DEADBEEF)
  DgCaseFill(64,   1,  64,   0, $00FF00FF)
  DgCaseFill(64,  17,  64,   0, $00FF00FF)
  DgCaseFill(64,  17, 256,   0, $00FF00FF)
  DgCaseFill(68,   5, 256,   0, $12345678)
  DgCaseFill(252,  9, 256,   0, $FFFFFFFF)
  DgCaseFill(256,  9, 256,   0, $01020304)
  DgCaseFill(256, 17, 512,   0, $01020304)
  DgCaseFill(1024, 8, 2048,  0, $CAFEF00D)

  ; The destination moved off the start of the buffer, so an engine that
  ; ignored the address entirely - or masked it - shows up.
  DgCaseFill(64,   4,  64, 128, $77777777)
  DgCaseFill(64,   4, 256, 128, $77777777)
  DgCaseFill(16,   4,  64,  32, $77777777)
  PutNl()
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; GROUP B - fill, awkward. Rows and destinations that are not
; four-byte aligned.
;
; The PDF says the engine "can deal with byte aligned transfers and
; will minimise bus traffic by buffering and packing misaligned
; accesses" (bcm2711-peripherals.txt:2286). These cases hold it to
; that. THEY ARE IN THEIR OWN GROUP because a failure here is a
; different finding from a failure in group A: it would mean "align
; your rectangles", which is a constraint dma.pi4 would then have to
; document and enforce, not evidence that anything is broken.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure DgGroupB()
  PutS("-- group B: fill, rows and destinations that are NOT four-byte aligned")
  PutNl()

  DgCaseFill(1,   1,  64,   0, $11223344)
  DgCaseFill(3,   1,  64,   0, $11223344)
  DgCaseFill(5,   4,  64,   0, $11223344)
  DgCaseFill(17,  4,  64,   0, $A5A5A5A5)
  DgCaseFill(63,  4,  64,   0, $A5A5A5A5)
  DgCaseFill(63,  4, 128,   0, $A5A5A5A5)
  DgCaseFill(64,  4,  67,   0, $DEADBEEF)   ; an odd PITCH, not an odd row
  DgCaseFill(16,  4,  64,   1, $12345678)
  DgCaseFill(16,  4,  64,   2, $12345678)
  DgCaseFill(16,  4,  64,   3, $12345678)
  DgCaseFill(17,  4,  67,   3, $12345678)
  PutNl()
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; GROUP C - copy. The same shapes with a real source.
;
; Half of these give the source a DIFFERENT pitch from the destination,
; which is the case that matters most: one 32-bit STRIDE register
; carries both, D_STRIDE in 31:16 and S_STRIDE in 15:0
; (bcm2711-peripherals.txt:3491-3515), and a packing error there
; produces a picture that looks almost right.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure DgGroupC()
  PutS("-- group C: copy, including source and destination pitches that differ")
  PutNl()

  DgCaseCopy(4,    1,   4,    4,   0)
  DgCaseCopy(16,   4,  16,   16,   0)
  DgCaseCopy(16,   4,  64,   16,   0)
  DgCaseCopy(16,   4,  16,   64,   0)
  DgCaseCopy(64,   8,  64,   64,   0)
  DgCaseCopy(64,   8, 256,   64,   0)
  DgCaseCopy(64,   8,  64,  256,   0)
  DgCaseCopy(68,   5, 256,  128,   0)
  DgCaseCopy(252,  9, 256,  512,   0)
  DgCaseCopy(256, 17, 512,  256,   0)
  DgCaseCopy(1024, 8, 2048, 1024,  0)
  DgCaseCopy(64,   4,  64,   64, 128)
  DgCaseCopy(17,   4,  67,   64,   3)
  DgCaseCopy(5,    4,  64,   64,   1)
  PutNl()
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; GROUP D - the overlapping downward copy. The one assumption.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure DgGroupD()
  PutS("-- group D: OVERLAPPING downward copy - the scroll, and the one assumption")
  PutNl()

  DgCaseScroll(64,  16,  64, 1)     ; contiguous, shift one row
  DgCaseScroll(64,  16,  64, 4)     ; shift four rows
  DgCaseScroll(64,  16, 128, 1)     ; with a gap between rows
  DgCaseScroll(1024, 8, 1024, 1)    ; rows longer than any burst
  DgCaseScroll(17,   9,  67, 2)     ; awkward everything
  PutNl()
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; GROUP E - the refusals.
;
; Nothing here touches the hardware. Every one of these is a rectangle
; that MUST be turned away before a register is written, and the reason
; given must be the right reason - a check that refuses for the wrong
; cause is a check that will wave through the case it was written for.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure DgGroupE()
  PutS("-- group E: the refusals that keep a bus master inside its window")
  PutNl()

  ; Outside the declared window, both ends.
  DgMustRefuse("a fill one byte below OUT", DmaFillPattern32(#DG_OUT - 1, 64, 64, 1, 0), #DMA_ERR_BOUNDS)
  DgMustRefuse("a fill whose last row runs past OUT", DmaFillPattern32((#DG_OUT + #DG_BYTES) - 64, 64, 64, 2, 0), #DMA_ERR_BOUNDS)
  DgMustRefuse("a fill aimed at REF, which is not the window", DmaFillPattern32(#DG_REF, 64, 64, 1, 0), #DMA_ERR_BOUNDS)
  DgMustRefuse("a copy aimed at SRC, which is not the window", DmaCopy2D(#DG_SRC, 64, #DG_REF, 64, 64, 1), #DMA_ERR_BOUNDS)

  ; Beyond what the register fields can express. Each of these would be
  ; TRUNCATED rather than refused if the check were missing, and a
  ; truncated XLENGTH is a fill of the wrong width that reads as a
  ; diagonal shear.
  DgMustRefuse("a row longer than XLENGTH can hold", DmaFillPattern32(#DG_OUT, 70000, 70000, 1, 0), #DMA_ERR_ARGS)
  DgMustRefuse("more rows than YLENGTH can hold", DmaFillPattern32(#DG_OUT, 4, 4, 20000, 0), #DMA_ERR_ARGS)
  DgMustRefuse("a gap wider than the signed stride field", DmaFillPattern32(#DG_OUT, 40000, 4, 2, 0), #DMA_ERR_STRIDE)
  DgMustRefuse("a pitch narrower than the row", DmaFillPattern32(#DG_OUT, 32, 64, 2, 0), #DMA_ERR_ARGS)
  DgMustRefuse("a zero-height rectangle", DmaFillPattern32(#DG_OUT, 64, 64, 0, 0), #DMA_ERR_ARGS)
  DgMustRefuse("a zero-width rectangle", DmaFillPattern32(#DG_OUT, 64, 0, 4, 0), #DMA_ERR_ARGS)

  ; Beyond the 1 GiB the engines can reach. This is the silent one.
  DgMustRefuse("a source above the 1 GiB cap", DmaCopy2D(#DG_OUT, 64, $80000000, 64, 64, 1), #DMA_ERR_REACH)

  ; The overlap the library will not do.
  DgMustRefuse("an overlapping UPWARD copy", DmaCopy2D(#DG_OUT + 64, 64, #DG_OUT, 64, 64, 8), #DMA_ERR_OVERLAP)

  ; THE BURST CAP, and this is the guard whose failure mode is a board
  ; that stops answering rather than a bad rectangle. Added 2026-08-26
  ; after group F locked the AXI bus. No transfer is attempted here -
  ; these are setter calls and nothing else - so this costs nothing and
  ; risks nothing.
  DgMustRefuse("DmaSetBurst refuses 15", DmaSetBurst(15), #DMA_ERR_BURST)
  DgMustRefuse("DmaSetBurst refuses one above the cap", DmaSetBurst(#DMA_BURST_MAX_SAFE + 1), #DMA_ERR_BURST)
  dgChecks = dgChecks + 1
  If DmaSetBurst(#DMA_BURST_MAX_SAFE) <> 1
    dgFails = dgFails + 1
    PutS("  FAIL  DmaSetBurst accepts the cap itself   it refused: ")
    PutS(DmaErrorText())
    PutNl()
  EndIf
  dgChecks = dgChecks + 1
  If DmaBurst() <> #DMA_BURST_MAX_SAFE
    dgFails = dgFails + 1
    PutS("  FAIL  a refused DmaSetBurst changed the burst anyway")
    PutNl()
  EndIf
  ; DmaSetBurstProbe()'s ACCEPTANCE above the cap is not tested here.
  ; Group F is the proof of it, and proving it here would leave the
  ; burst set above the cap in a group that is supposed to be inert.

  PutNl()
EndProcedure


; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; GROUP A2 - THE BRACKET. Added 2026-08-26, after the bench.
;
; The first run failed exactly one case, `w=1024 h=8 dp=2048`, and every
; other group A fill passed. The widest of those was w=256. So the
; boundary was somewhere in 256 < w <= 1024 and nothing in the suite
; said where. This group says where.
;
; ---- WHAT IT SEPARATES, AND THIS IS THE POINT OF THE LAYOUT ----------
; The failing case had TWO quantities that were unusually large and no
; other case had either: a 1024-byte ROW and a 1024-byte STRIDE. One
; test cannot tell them apart, so this group runs each ladder with the
; other quantity pinned:
;
;   THE ROW LADDER runs at PITCH = ROWBYTES, so the destination stride
;   is ZERO on every rung. If the row ladder fails at some width, the
;   stride is not involved, because there isn't one.
;
;   THE STRIDE LADDER holds the row at a width the first run already
;   proved good - 64 bytes - and walks the stride up past 1024 to 4096.
;   If the stride ladder is clean, D_STRIDE is exonerated outright.
;
;   THE CORNER re-runs the original failing geometry, so the fix is
;   asserted against the exact case that found the bug rather than
;   against a case that resembles it.
;
; ---- WHAT THE DIAGNOSIS PREDICTS -------------------------------------
; The cause was found to be the fill's SOURCE, not its length and not
; its stride: the pattern was 32 bytes, a read burst is up to 256, and
; the engine read past the end of the pattern into memory nothing had
; written. The decisive evidence was that the bad byte was $00 and this
; gate seeds with $3C, so the engine WROTE there - a truncated length
; would have left $3C behind and a bad stride would have put good data
; somewhere else.
;
; SO THE PREDICTION IS: with the pattern at 256 bytes, EVERY RUNG OF
; BOTH LADDERS PASSES, including w=2048 and w=4096 which are wider than
; anything the original suite tried.
;
; IF A ROW RUNG STILL FAILS, the diagnosis was wrong or incomplete and
; the boundary is real. Report the largest width that passed: that
; number goes into #DMA_FILL_MAX_ROW_BYTES in dma.pi4, which already
; refuses anything above it with #DMA_ERR_ROWLIMIT. A silently short
; fill on a framebuffer is a partially drawn frame that reads as a
; rendering bug three layers up, so it must be a named refusal and not
; a comment.
;
; IF A STRIDE RUNG FAILS INSTEAD, the fault is in the STRIDE word this
; file packs - D_STRIDE in 31:16, S_STRIDE in 15:0
; (bcm2711-peripherals.txt:3491-3515) - and dma.pi4's DmaGeomOk() needs
; a tighter cap than the field's own 32767.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure DgGroupA2()
  PutS("-- group A2: the bracket. Row length and stride walked separately.")
  PutNl()

  ; The row ladder. Pitch equals the row, so the stride is zero
  ; throughout and only the row length is changing.
  DgCaseFill(256,   4,  256,  0, $CAFEF00D)
  DgCaseFill(320,   4,  320,  0, $CAFEF00D)
  DgCaseFill(384,   4,  384,  0, $CAFEF00D)
  DgCaseFill(512,   4,  512,  0, $CAFEF00D)
  DgCaseFill(640,   4,  640,  0, $CAFEF00D)
  DgCaseFill(768,   4,  768,  0, $CAFEF00D)
  DgCaseFill(896,   4,  896,  0, $CAFEF00D)
  DgCaseFill(1024,  4, 1024,  0, $CAFEF00D)
  DgCaseFill(2048,  4, 2048,  0, $CAFEF00D)
  DgCaseFill(4096,  4, 4096,  0, $CAFEF00D)

  ; The stride ladder. The row stays at 64, which the first run proved
  ; good, and the gap between rows walks past the value the failing
  ; case had.
  DgCaseFill(64,    8,   64,  0, $CAFEF00D)
  DgCaseFill(64,    8,  128,  0, $CAFEF00D)
  DgCaseFill(64,    8,  320,  0, $CAFEF00D)
  DgCaseFill(64,    8, 1088,  0, $CAFEF00D)   ; stride 1024, the suspect
  DgCaseFill(64,    8, 2048,  0, $CAFEF00D)   ; stride 1984
  DgCaseFill(64,    8, 4096,  0, $CAFEF00D)   ; stride 4032

  ; Both large at once, which is the original failing geometry, and
  ; then the same shape at an offset so the fix is not accidentally
  ; depending on the destination starting at the buffer base.
  DgCaseFill(1024,  8, 2048,  0, $CAFEF00D)   ; THE CASE THAT FAILED
  DgCaseFill(1024,  8, 2048, 256, $CAFEF00D)
  DgCaseFill(1024,  6, 2048, 128, $A5A5A5A5)
  PutNl()
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
; GROUP G - CACHE COHERENCY, AND THE ONLY GROUP WITH NEGATIVE CONTROLS
; ======================================================================
; Added 2026-08-27, when display.pi4 gained a DMA path and the question
; stopped being academic.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; WHY THE OTHER SIX GROUPS CANNOT ANSWER THIS QUESTION
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; Look at DgCaseFill above. It calls DmaCacheRange over the WHOLE 64 KiB
; before the transfer and again after it. That is correct, generous, and
; it makes the cache discipline UNFALSIFIABLE: those two calls alone
; satisfy every requirement the transfer has, so the case would pass
; with every cache operation inside dma.pi4 deleted, and it would pass
; with every cache operation inside display.pi4 deleted too.
;
; A test whose own hygiene supplies the property under test is not
; testing that property. Groups A to E prove GEOMETRY - strides,
; YLENGTH, packing, bounds, refusals - and they prove it well. They
; prove nothing at all about coherency and this group exists to say so
; out loud rather than let a green run imply otherwise.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; AND NEITHER CAN ANY EMULATOR, EVER
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; tools/a64/a64_interp.py runs the real compiled image against one flat
; byte array. No data cache, no dirty line, no eviction, no speculative
; refill, no second bus master. In that model the CPU's stores and the
; engine's stores land in the same array in program order, so none of
; the hazards below can be represented, let alone caught. Every case in
; this group would pass there with the maintenance removed.
;
; That is the same defect that let tools/a64/a64_sdio_check.py certify a
; payload which faults on silicon, because its memory was a dictionary
; that did not care about alignment. A model more forgiving than the
; part certifies bugs. COHERENCY IS PROVED ON SILICON OR IT IS NOT
; PROVED, and this group only means anything with #DG_MMU = 1.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE THREE HAZARDS, AND WHAT EACH CASE DOES ABOUT IT
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;   HAZARD A - the engine READS what the CPU left in a dirty line, so it
;              copies whatever DRAM last saw. G4 omits the source clean
;              and must come out WRONG; G5 supplies it and must come out
;              right.
;
;   HAZARD B - the CPU READS a stale but clean line over pixels the
;              engine wrote. G3 omits the post-transfer invalidate, with
;              the lines deliberately primed resident first, and must
;              come out WRONG.
;
;   HAZARD C - a DIRTY line the CPU left over the DESTINATION is evicted
;              AFTER the engine's write and puts the old pixels back.
;              This is the worst of the three: it succeeds, then
;              corrupts, and it does it at a time of the cache's
;              choosing. G2 omits the pre-transfer clean and then FORCES
;              the eviction with DgEvict(), and must come out WRONG.
;              G1 supplies the clean and must come out right.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; WHY THE NEGATIVE CONTROLS ARE THE IMPORTANT HALF
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; A coherency test that only ever asserts "with the maintenance, it
; worked" cannot distinguish a correct discipline from a machine where
; the caches happened not to matter that afternoon - a run with the MMU
; off, a run where the working set stayed resident, a run where the
; compiler hoisted the read. Every one of those makes the positive cases
; green and the library unproven.
;
; So each hazard is asserted in BOTH directions and the negative comes
; first in the reading order. If a negative control passes, its positive
; twin is downgraded to INCONCLUSIVE in the summary, because it is.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE GEOMETRY IS SMALL ON PURPOSE
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; 64 bytes by 16 rows at a pitch of 64 - one contiguous kilobyte. G3
; needs its primed lines to still be resident when it reads them back,
; and a kilobyte fits in the 32 KiB L1 with room to spare. A large
; rectangle would evict itself during the priming loop and G3 would pass
; for the wrong reason, which is exactly the failure this group is built
; to refuse to make.
; ======================================================================

#DG_G_ROWBYTES = 64
#DG_G_ROWS     = 16
#DG_G_PITCH    = 64
#DG_G_BYTES    = 1024          ; = ROWBYTES * ROWS, the contiguous span

; The two patterns. They must differ in EVERY byte, so that a stale
; readback of any byte at all is a mismatch rather than a coincidence.
;   $CAFEF00D is what the engine is asked to write.
;   $13572468 is what the CPU leaves behind in the dirty lines.
#DG_G_ENGINE = $CAFEF00D
#DG_G_STALE  = $13572468

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; DgEvict - push everything out of the caches, on demand.
;
; Read one word out of every 64-byte line across two megabytes. A load
; allocates the whole containing line, so by the end the L1 and the L2
; hold nothing but this region and every dirty line that was over the
; gate's buffers has been WRITTEN BACK TO DRAM on its way out.
;
; THE WRITE-BACK IS THE POINT, not the eviction. Hazard C is not "the
; line went away", it is "the line went away and took its old contents
; with it, over the top of the engine's work". This procedure is the
; only thing in the gate that can make that happen at a known moment.
;
; THE RESULT IS ACCUMULATED AND RETURNED, and that is not decoration.
; A loop whose loads feed nothing is a loop a compiler may remove. This
; backend does not do that today - it has no such pass - but a read loop
; that cannot be elided is cheap insurance against the day it does, and
; the day it does would silently turn every case below green.
;
; PeekN, the unsigned 32-bit read: the value is never interpreted, only
; summed, but a widening read would touch two lines per step and halve
; the region actually walked.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i DgEvict()
  Define i.i
  Define acc.i
  acc = 0
  i = 0
  While i < #DG_THRASH_BYTES
    acc = acc + (PeekN(#DG_THRASH + i) & $FF)
    i = i + 64
  Wend
  ProcedureReturn acc
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; DgPrime - read a region so its lines are resident and CLEAN.
;
; For hazard B. A clean line is not a line the CPU is entitled to
; ignore: the engine will write DRAM underneath it and the CPU will keep
; serving the old value out of the cache until somebody invalidates.
; This is how the gate guarantees the line is there to be stale, instead
; of hoping a prefetcher put one there.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i DgPrime(addr.i, bytes.i)
  Define i.i
  Define acc.i
  acc = 0
  i = 0
  While i < bytes
    acc = acc + (PeekN(addr + i) & $FF)
    i = i + 64
  Wend
  ProcedureReturn acc
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; DgExpectSame / DgExpectDiff - the two verdicts this group can reach.
;
; DgExpectSame is an ordinary check: a mismatch is a FAIL.
;
; DgExpectDiff is the negative control and it is NOT an ordinary check
; inverted. A match there is not a defect in dma.pi4 - it means the
; hazard did not reproduce, so this run has learned nothing about the
; discipline in either direction. It is counted as INCONCLUSIVE and it
; says, in the line it prints, exactly which positive case it has just
; invalidated. Reporting it as a pass would be the single most
; misleading thing this file could do.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure DgExpectSame(*name, diff.i)
  dgChecks = dgChecks + 1
  If diff < 0
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  dgFails = dgFails + 1
  PutS("  FAIL  ")
  PutS(*name)
  PutS("   first bad byte at +")
  PrintDec(diff)
  PutS(":  want $")
  PutHexN(PeekB(#DG_REF + diff) & $FF, 2)
  PutS("  got $")
  PutHexN(PeekB(#DG_OUT + diff) & $FF, 2)
  PutNl()
EndProcedure

Procedure DgExpectDiff(*name, diff.i)
  dgChecks = dgChecks + 1
  If diff >= 0
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  dgIncon = dgIncon + 1
  PutS("  INCONCLUSIVE  ")
  PutS(*name)
  PutNl()
  PutS("      the omitted cache operation made NO difference on this run, so the")
  PutNl()
  PutS("      hazard did not reproduce and the matching positive case proves")
  PutNl()
  PutS("      nothing. Check #DG_MMU is 1 and that MmuEnabled() said so above.")
  PutNl()
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; DgGroupG - the six cases.
;
; EVERY CASE BUILDS ITS OWN REFERENCE WITH THE CPU BYTE LOOP, exactly as
; the rest of the gate does, so the oracle is still fifteen lines of
; PokeB that share nothing with dma.pi4.
;
; THE COMPARISON IS OVER THE WHOLE 64 KiB, as everywhere else here. A
; cache line is 64 bytes and a rectangle edge is not on one, so a
; maintenance bug that over-reaches damages the bytes NEXT to the
; rectangle - which is precisely what a rectangle-only comparison cannot
; see. That is the same argument the file's header makes for the
; geometry groups and it is stronger here, not weaker.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure DgGroupG()
  Define acc.i
  Define d.i

  PutS("-- group G: CACHE COHERENCY. Three hazards, each asserted BOTH ways.")
  PutNl()

  If #DG_MMU = 0
    PutS("   SKIPPED - #DG_MMU is 0, so DRAM is uncached, every cache operation")
    PutNl()
    PutS("   in this program is architecturally a no-op, and there is no hazard")
    PutNl()
    PutS("   to provoke. This group is only meaningful with the caches ON.")
    PutNl()
    PutNl()
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf

  acc = 0

  ; ==================================================================
  ; G1 - HAZARD C, THE DISCIPLINE APPLIED. Must come out RIGHT.
  ;
  ; The CPU dirties the destination, the discipline cleans and
  ; invalidates it, the engine fills it, the post-pass invalidates it,
  ; and only THEN is an eviction forced. There is nothing dirty left to
  ; evict, so the engine's pixels survive.
  ;
  ; THE MAINTENANCE HERE IS DmaCacheRect OVER THE RECTANGLE ONLY, not
  ; DmaCacheRange over the whole buffer. That is deliberate and it puts
  ; the new procedure on the critical path of a case that fails if it
  ; under-maintains by so much as one line - which is the only way a
  ; cache-maintenance routine can be tested at all.
  ; ==================================================================
  DgSet(#DG_REF, #DG_BYTES, $3C)
  DgSet(#DG_OUT, #DG_BYTES, $3C)
  DgFillRef(#DG_REF, #DG_G_PITCH, #DG_G_ROWBYTES, #DG_G_ROWS, #DG_G_ENGINE)
  ; The CPU leaves dirty lines holding a DIFFERENT pattern over the
  ; destination. This is the thing that must not come back.
  DgFillRef(#DG_OUT, #DG_G_PITCH, #DG_G_ROWBYTES, #DG_G_ROWS, #DG_G_STALE)

  DmaCacheRect(#DG_OUT, #DG_G_PITCH, #DG_G_ROWBYTES, #DG_G_ROWS)
  If DmaFillPattern32(#DG_OUT, #DG_G_PITCH, #DG_G_ROWBYTES, #DG_G_ROWS, #DG_G_ENGINE) = 0
    dgChecks = dgChecks + 1
    dgFails  = dgFails + 1
    PutS("  FAIL  G1  the fill was REFUSED: ")
    PutS(DmaErrorText())
    PutNl()
  Else
    DmaCacheRect(#DG_OUT, #DG_G_PITCH, #DG_G_ROWBYTES, #DG_G_ROWS)
    acc = acc + DgEvict()
    DmaCacheRange(#DG_OUT, #DG_BYTES)
    d = DgDiff(#DG_REF, #DG_OUT, #DG_BYTES)
    DgExpectSame("G1  hazard C, clean before + invalidate after, then evicted", d)
  EndIf

  ; ==================================================================
  ; G2 - HAZARD C, THE NEGATIVE CONTROL. Must come out WRONG.
  ;
  ; Identical to G1 with the PRE-transfer clean removed. The CPU's dirty
  ; lines are still sitting over the destination when the engine writes
  ; DRAM; DgEvict() then writes them back on top and the picture reverts
  ; to what the CPU had put there.
  ;
  ; IF THIS PASSES - that is, if the two buffers still agree - the run
  ; could not provoke the hazard and G1 proved nothing.
  ; ==================================================================
  DgSet(#DG_REF, #DG_BYTES, $3C)
  DgSet(#DG_OUT, #DG_BYTES, $3C)
  DgFillRef(#DG_REF, #DG_G_PITCH, #DG_G_ROWBYTES, #DG_G_ROWS, #DG_G_ENGINE)
  DgFillRef(#DG_OUT, #DG_G_PITCH, #DG_G_ROWBYTES, #DG_G_ROWS, #DG_G_STALE)

  ; NO DmaCacheRect HERE. That omission is the whole case.
  If DmaFillPattern32(#DG_OUT, #DG_G_PITCH, #DG_G_ROWBYTES, #DG_G_ROWS, #DG_G_ENGINE) = 0
    dgChecks = dgChecks + 1
    dgFails  = dgFails + 1
    PutS("  FAIL  G2  the fill was REFUSED: ")
    PutS(DmaErrorText())
    PutNl()
  Else
    acc = acc + DgEvict()
    ; Invalidate before comparing, so the comparison reads DRAM - which
    ; is where the damage is - rather than a cache that may still be
    ; telling the CPU its own story.
    DmaCacheRange(#DG_OUT, #DG_BYTES)
    d = DgDiff(#DG_REF, #DG_OUT, #DG_BYTES)
    DgExpectDiff("G2  hazard C, NO clean before the transfer, then evicted", d)
  EndIf

  ; ==================================================================
  ; G3 - HAZARD B, THE NEGATIVE CONTROL. Must come out WRONG.
  ;
  ; The destination is made clean and consistent with DRAM, then READ so
  ; that clean lines are resident. The engine writes DRAM underneath
  ; them. With no invalidate the CPU keeps serving the old value out of
  ; the cache, so the comparison sees the pre-fill contents.
  ;
  ; NO DgEvict() IN THIS ONE, and that is the difference from G2: the
  ; whole point is that the lines are STILL THERE. Evicting would fix
  ; the very thing being demonstrated.
  ; ==================================================================
  DgSet(#DG_REF, #DG_BYTES, $3C)
  DgSet(#DG_OUT, #DG_BYTES, $3C)
  DgFillRef(#DG_REF, #DG_G_PITCH, #DG_G_ROWBYTES, #DG_G_ROWS, #DG_G_ENGINE)
  DmaCacheRange(#DG_OUT, #DG_BYTES)
  acc = acc + DgPrime(#DG_OUT, #DG_G_BYTES)

  If DmaFillPattern32(#DG_OUT, #DG_G_PITCH, #DG_G_ROWBYTES, #DG_G_ROWS, #DG_G_ENGINE) = 0
    dgChecks = dgChecks + 1
    dgFails  = dgFails + 1
    PutS("  FAIL  G3  the fill was REFUSED: ")
    PutS(DmaErrorText())
    PutNl()
  Else
    ; NO invalidate. The compare below is the CPU reading its own stale
    ; lines, which is the hazard.
    d = DgDiff(#DG_REF, #DG_OUT, #DG_BYTES)
    DgExpectDiff("G3  hazard B, primed clean lines and NO invalidate after", d)
  EndIf

  ; ==================================================================
  ; G4 - HAZARD B, THE DISCIPLINE APPLIED. Must come out RIGHT.
  ;
  ; The same as G3 with the post-transfer invalidate put back. One line
  ; of difference, and the whole hazard.
  ; ==================================================================
  DgSet(#DG_REF, #DG_BYTES, $3C)
  DgSet(#DG_OUT, #DG_BYTES, $3C)
  DgFillRef(#DG_REF, #DG_G_PITCH, #DG_G_ROWBYTES, #DG_G_ROWS, #DG_G_ENGINE)
  DmaCacheRange(#DG_OUT, #DG_BYTES)
  acc = acc + DgPrime(#DG_OUT, #DG_G_BYTES)

  If DmaFillPattern32(#DG_OUT, #DG_G_PITCH, #DG_G_ROWBYTES, #DG_G_ROWS, #DG_G_ENGINE) = 0
    dgChecks = dgChecks + 1
    dgFails  = dgFails + 1
    PutS("  FAIL  G4  the fill was REFUSED: ")
    PutS(DmaErrorText())
    PutNl()
  Else
    DmaCacheRect(#DG_OUT, #DG_G_PITCH, #DG_G_ROWBYTES, #DG_G_ROWS)
    d = DgDiff(#DG_REF, #DG_OUT, #DG_BYTES)
    DgExpectSame("G4  hazard B, primed clean lines and the invalidate applied", d)
  EndIf

  ; ==================================================================
  ; G5 - HAZARD A, THE NEGATIVE CONTROL. Must come out WRONG.
  ;
  ; The CPU writes a source rectangle and does NOT clean it. The engine
  ; reads DRAM, which still holds what was there before, and copies
  ; that. The destination therefore ends up holding the OLD source, not
  ; the new one.
  ;
  ; THE DESTINATION SIDE IS FULLY MAINTAINED so that the only variable
  ; is the source. If both sides were left alone a failure could not be
  ; attributed to either.
  ; ==================================================================
  DgSet(#DG_REF, #DG_BYTES, $3C)
  DgSet(#DG_OUT, #DG_BYTES, $3C)
  DgSet(#DG_SRC, #DG_BYTES, $3C)
  DmaCacheRange(#DG_SRC, #DG_BYTES)          ; SRC and DRAM now agree on $3C

  ; The reference is what the CPU intends the copy to deliver.
  DgFillRef(#DG_REF, #DG_G_PITCH, #DG_G_ROWBYTES, #DG_G_ROWS, #DG_G_STALE)
  ; The same bytes into the real source - left DIRTY on purpose.
  DgFillRef(#DG_SRC, #DG_G_PITCH, #DG_G_ROWBYTES, #DG_G_ROWS, #DG_G_STALE)

  DmaCacheRange(#DG_OUT, #DG_BYTES)
  If DmaCopy2D(#DG_OUT, #DG_G_PITCH, #DG_SRC, #DG_G_PITCH, #DG_G_ROWBYTES, #DG_G_ROWS) = 0
    dgChecks = dgChecks + 1
    dgFails  = dgFails + 1
    PutS("  FAIL  G5  the copy was REFUSED: ")
    PutS(DmaErrorText())
    PutNl()
  Else
    DmaCacheRange(#DG_OUT, #DG_BYTES)
    d = DgDiff(#DG_REF, #DG_OUT, #DG_BYTES)
    DgExpectDiff("G5  hazard A, source left dirty and NOT cleaned before the copy", d)
  EndIf

  ; ==================================================================
  ; G6 - HAZARD A, THE DISCIPLINE APPLIED. Must come out RIGHT.
  ; ==================================================================
  DgSet(#DG_REF, #DG_BYTES, $3C)
  DgSet(#DG_OUT, #DG_BYTES, $3C)
  DgSet(#DG_SRC, #DG_BYTES, $3C)
  DmaCacheRange(#DG_SRC, #DG_BYTES)

  DgFillRef(#DG_REF, #DG_G_PITCH, #DG_G_ROWBYTES, #DG_G_ROWS, #DG_G_STALE)
  DgFillRef(#DG_SRC, #DG_G_PITCH, #DG_G_ROWBYTES, #DG_G_ROWS, #DG_G_STALE)

  DmaCacheRect(#DG_SRC, #DG_G_PITCH, #DG_G_ROWBYTES, #DG_G_ROWS)   ; the clean
  DmaCacheRange(#DG_OUT, #DG_BYTES)
  If DmaCopy2D(#DG_OUT, #DG_G_PITCH, #DG_SRC, #DG_G_PITCH, #DG_G_ROWBYTES, #DG_G_ROWS) = 0
    dgChecks = dgChecks + 1
    dgFails  = dgFails + 1
    PutS("  FAIL  G6  the copy was REFUSED: ")
    PutS(DmaErrorText())
    PutNl()
  Else
    DmaCacheRange(#DG_OUT, #DG_BYTES)
    d = DgDiff(#DG_REF, #DG_OUT, #DG_BYTES)
    DgExpectSame("G6  hazard A, source cleaned with DmaCacheRect before the copy", d)
  EndIf

  ; The accumulator exists so the read loops cannot be optimised away.
  ; Printing it is what makes that true rather than merely intended, and
  ; the value itself carries no information at all - do not read it as a
  ; result.
  PutS("   (eviction and priming walked ")
  PrintDec(acc)
  PutS(" bytes' worth of low bytes - the number is")
  PutNl()
  PutS("    printed only so the read loops cannot be dead code)")
  PutNl()
  PutNl()
EndProcedure


; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; GROUP F - THE BURST CEILING. THIS GROUP IS EXPECTED TO HANG THE BOARD.
;
; ======================================================================
; READ THIS BEFORE RUNNING IT
; ======================================================================
; ARM THE DEADMAN. `deadman 15` at the Anvil prompt, then `run`. On
; 2026-08-26 this group locked the AXI bus and the deadman is what got
; the board back; without it the recovery is hands on the hardware. The
; counter is 20 bits at 65536 Hz so ~15.999 s is the ceiling on this
; part (safety.pi4:491-498 clamps and returns the real value), which is
; why every rung below is ONE case and not three.
;
; NOTHING ELSE IN THIS FILE CAN DO THIS. Groups A to E run inside
; DmaSetBurst()'s cap, which is #DMA_BURST_MAX_SAFE = 8 and is a
; refusal, not a clamp. This group is the single caller of
; DmaSetBurstProbe(), the door in dma.pi4 that exists so a ladder can
; climb past the cap and find out where the cap belongs.
;
; ======================================================================
; WHAT HAPPENED, AND WHY THE LADDER NOW RUNS DOWNWARDS
; ======================================================================
; The first version of this group walked UP from burst 0 and printed
; only failures. It hung, and the transcript could not say which rung
; it hung on, because a rung that hangs never reaches its own failure
; line. That is one wasted reset and no information.
;
; Two things changed.
;
;   IT ANNOUNCES EACH RUNG BEFORE ATTEMPTING IT, AND DRAINS THE UART.
;   The drain is the part that matters: without it the line sits in the
;   PL011's transmit FIFO while the engine starts, and if the bus locks
;   the CPU never runs the code that would have pushed it out. So the
;   marker naming the rung would die in the FIFO along with everything
;   else. Announce, drain, then touch the hardware. The last line on
;   the wire is then the answer.
;
;   IT WALKS DOWN FROM 15, NOT UP FROM 0. A hang ends the run, so a
;   ladder yields exactly ONE data point per reset whichever way it
;   runs - and the interesting end is the top. Walking up meant paying
;   for every safe rung before reaching the one in question. Walking
;   down puts the answer in the first rung or two.
;
; ======================================================================
; HOW TO FIND THE EXACT RUNG IN ONE RUN
; ======================================================================
; The ladder WALKS UP, and a hanging rung ends the run, so the run
; stops at the LOWEST burst length that hangs - which is the ceiling.
; The last "burst N survived" line names the highest safe value and the
; "attempting" line under it names the one that locked the bus.
;
; That is one run and one reset, not a bisection. An earlier version of
; this header described a three-run bisection over 9..15; it walked the
; ladder downwards, where the run stops at the HIGHEST hanging rung and
; learns nothing about the six below it. See the comment on the loop.
;
; #DG_F_TOP and #DG_F_BOTTOM still bound the ladder and SETTING THEM
; EQUAL STILL RUNS A SINGLE RUNG, which is the right tool for
; re-testing one value - just not for finding the ceiling.
;
; WHEN THE NUMBER IS KNOWN: set #DMA_BURST_MAX_SAFE in dma.pi4 to one
; BELOW the lowest hanging rung, note the bench date beside it, and
; DmaSetBurst() refuses the rest with #DMA_ERR_BURST from then on. If
; the answer comes back as 9, the constant stays at 8 and this ladder
; will have confirmed the cap rather than moved it - which is still
; worth knowing, because "8 because the FIFO is 8 words" and "8 because
; 9 hangs" are different degrees of confidence.
;
; ======================================================================
; WHAT IS ACTUALLY BEING TESTED AT EACH RUNG
; ======================================================================
; Two questions at once, and they fail differently:
;
;   DOES IT HANG?  The bus-lock question. Answered by whether the next
;                  line appears.
;   DOES IT READ PAST THE 256-BYTE PATTERN?  The regression question -
;                  the pattern is 256 bytes because sixteen beats of
;                  128 bits is the documented burst ceiling
;                  (bcm2711-peripherals.txt:5231), so no legal burst
;                  length should be able to walk off the end of it.
;                  Answered by the byte-for-byte compare, which prints
;                  a FAIL line like any other case.
;
; A rung that prints its announcement and then a FAIL is the second
; problem. A rung that prints its announcement and then nothing is the
; first.
;
; One fill case per rung, at w=1024 h=8 dp=2048 - the geometry that
; found the original bug - so a rung is a few tens of milliseconds and
; the whole ladder fits inside the deadman with room to spare.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#DG_F_TOP    = 15
#DG_F_BOTTOM = 8

Procedure DgGroupF()
  Define n.i
  Define save.i

  save = DmaBurst()

  PutS("-- group F: the burst ceiling. THIS GROUP IS EXPECTED TO HANG THE BOARD.")
  PutNl()
  PutS("   Every rung is named BEFORE it is attempted and the UART is drained, so")
  PutNl()
  PutS("   the LAST LINE ON THE WIRE IS THE RUNG THAT HUNG. Walking DOWN from ")
  PrintDec(#DG_F_TOP)
  PutS(" to ")
  PrintDec(#DG_F_BOTTOM)
  PutS(".")
  PutNl()
  PutS("   Set #DG_F_TOP and #DG_F_BOTTOM equal to run one rung and bisect.")
  PutNl()
  UartDrain()

  ; THE LADDER WALKS UP, AND THE FIRST VERSION WALKED DOWN ON MY OWN
  ; BAD ADVICE. The reasoning for walking down was that if the ceiling
  ; is near the top, walking up pays for the whole ladder before
  ; reaching the interesting rung. That is true of a ladder whose rungs
  ; all run - and false of this one, because A HANGING RUNG ENDS THE
  ; RUN. The board resets and nothing after it executes.
  ;
  ; So the two directions are not symmetric:
  ;
  ;   walking DOWN from 15   stops at the HIGHEST hanging rung, which
  ;                          is 15 itself if 15 hangs. One data point,
  ;                          and the six rungs below it never ran.
  ;   walking UP from 8      stops at the LOWEST hanging rung, which is
  ;                          exactly the ceiling being looked for.
  ;
  ; Walking up answers the question in ONE run. The bisection below is
  ; kept because setting TOP and BOTTOM equal is still the right tool
  ; for re-testing a single rung, but it is no longer the way to find
  ; the ceiling from scratch.
  ;
  ; The rungs cost tens of milliseconds each, so paying for the whole
  ; ladder was never the expensive part. A reset is.
  n = #DG_F_BOTTOM
  While n <= #DG_F_TOP
    ; The rung's own marker. Printed and DRAINED before anything is
    ; asked of the engine, which is the whole instrument.
    UartWriteStr("  .. burst ")
    PrintDec(n)
    UartWriteStr("  attempting w=1024 h=8 dp=2048 ...")
    PutNl()
    UartDrain()

    ; DmaSetBurstProbe, not DmaSetBurst: above #DMA_BURST_MAX_SAFE the
    ; ordinary setter refuses with #DMA_ERR_BURST, which is correct for
    ; every caller except this one.
    If DmaSetBurstProbe(n) = 0
      PutS("  FAIL  burst ")
      PrintDec(n)
      PutS("  DmaSetBurstProbe refused: ")
      PutS(DmaErrorText())
      PutNl()
      UartDrain()
    Else
      DgCaseFill(1024, 8, 2048, 0, $CAFEF00D)
      ; Reaching here at all is the pass. Say so, because in THIS group
      ; silence is the thing being watched for and an unbroken run of
      ; announcements with nothing between them is ambiguous.
      UartWriteStr("     burst ")
      PrintDec(n)
      UartWriteStr("  survived")
      PutNl()
      UartDrain()
    EndIf

    n = n + 1
  Wend

  DmaSetBurst(save)
  PutS("   group F completed without hanging. Every rung from ")
  PrintDec(#DG_F_BOTTOM)
  PutS(" up to ")
  PrintDec(#DG_F_TOP)
  PutNl()
  PutS("   survived, so #DMA_BURST_MAX_SAFE could be raised to ")
  PrintDec(#DG_F_TOP)
  PutS(" - but only")
  PutNl()
  PutS("   on the strength of more than one run, and only on channel 0.")
  PutNl()
  UartDrain()
  PutNl()
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  MAIN
; ======================================================================
Procedure.i Main()
  UartInit(115200)

  PutNl()
  PutS("=== pi4DmaGate - the DMA engine against a CPU byte loop, byte for byte ===")
  PutNl()
  PutS("No framebuffer, no mailbox, no HDMI display needed. Three 64 KiB buffers")
  PutNl()
  PutS("at $780000, $790000 and $7A0000, and only the middle one is inside the")
  PutNl()
  PutS("window the library is allowed to write into.")
  PutNl()
  PutNl()

  ; MmuBuildTables RETURNS 0 ON SUCCESS and a negative #MMU_ERR_* on
  ; refusal (mmu.pi4:1044-1046). Testing it the other way round has
  ; already cost this project a run, because the output looks complete:
  ; the whole gate quietly runs with the caches OFF, where the cache
  ; maintenance it is supposed to be exercising is a no-op, and passes.
  If #DG_MMU = 1
    PutS("MMU: building the identity map at $600000 ...")
    PutNl()
    If MmuBuildTables(#DG_TABLES) <> 0
      PutS("MMU: the tables would not build. THE GATE IS NOW RUNNING UNCACHED,")
      PutNl()
      PutS("     which means every DmaCacheRange() below is a no-op and the cache")
      PutNl()
      PutS("     discipline is NOT being tested. A pass proves less than it looks.")
      PutNl()
    Else
      MmuEnable()
      If MmuEnabled() = 1
        PutS("MMU: ON, DRAM Normal Write-Back Cacheable. The cache discipline is")
        PutNl()
        PutS("     under test as well as the transfers.")
        PutNl()
      Else
        PutS("MMU: the enable did not take - running UNCACHED, see above.")
        PutNl()
      EndIf
    EndIf
  Else
    PutS("MMU: off by #DG_MMU. The transfers are under test; the cache discipline")
    PutNl()
    PutS("     is not, because every maintenance operation is a no-op here.")
    PutNl()
  EndIf
  PutNl()

  ; FORGET THE KNOWN ANSWER AND MEASURE IT AGAIN. dma.pi4 now starts
  ; with #DMA_YLENGTH_BIAS_BCM2711 = 1, measured on this board on
  ; 2026-08-26, and DmaInit() takes it as given so an ordinary program
  ; does not pay for a probe whose result is written down. A GATE IS
  ; NOT AN ORDINARY PROGRAM: the whole reason it exists is to re-derive
  ; what everything else assumes, so it puts the bias back to "unknown"
  ; and makes DmaInit() run the probe on every single bench run.
  DmaSetYLengthBias(-1)

  If DmaSetScratch(#DG_SCRATCH, #DG_SCRATCH_BYTES) = 0
    PutS("!! DmaSetScratch: ")
    PutS(DmaErrorText())
    PutNl()
    UartDrain()
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf

  ; OUT and nothing else. REF and SRC stay outside the window for the
  ; whole run, which is how group E's last four cases mean anything.
  If DmaSetBounds(#DG_OUT, #DG_BYTES) = 0
    PutS("!! DmaSetBounds: ")
    PutS(DmaErrorText())
    PutNl()
    UartDrain()
    ProcedureReturn 2
  EndIf

  If DmaInit() = 0
    PutS("!! DmaInit: ")
    PutS(DmaErrorText())
    PutNl()
    PutS("   Nothing below this line can run.")
    PutNl()
    UartDrain()
    If #DG_MMU = 1
      If MmuEnabled() = 1
        MmuDisable()
      EndIf
    EndIf
    ProcedureReturn 3
  EndIf

  PutS("dma: channel ")
  PrintDec(DmaChannel())
  PutS(", burst ")
  PrintDec(DmaBurst())
  PutS(", YLENGTH bias ")
  PrintDec(DmaYLengthBias())
  PutS(" (source advanced ")
  PrintDec(DmaCalibrateAdvance())
  PutS(" bytes in the probe)")
  PutNl()
  If DmaYLengthBias() = 0
    PutS("     YLENGTH is a PLAIN ROW COUNT on this part.")
  Else
    PutS("     YLENGTH is ROWS MINUS ONE on this part.")
  EndIf
  PutNl()
  PutNl()

  If #DG_TRACE = 1
    PutS("TRACING IS ON. Every case names itself before it runs and the UART is")
    PutNl()
    PutS("drained, so if the board stops the last line is the case that stopped it.")
    PutNl()
  Else
    PutS("Only failures print below. Silence inside groups A to E is those groups")
    PutNl()
    PutS("passing - they run inside DmaSetBurst's cap and none of them has hung.")
    PutNl()
    PutS("IF THE BOARD HANGS IN ONE OF THEM, re-run once with #DG_TRACE = 1; that")
    PutNl()
    PutS("is the whole recovery procedure. Group F always traces itself.")
    PutNl()
  EndIf
  PutNl()
  PutS("Group F runs LAST and is expected to hang the board. Arm the deadman.")
  PutNl()
  PutNl()
  UartDrain()

  DgGroupA()
  DgGroupA2()
  DgGroupB()
  DgGroupC()
  DgGroupD()
  DgGroupE()
  ; G BEFORE F, ALWAYS. Group F is expected to hang the board and a group
  ; queued after it may never run at all. Coherency is the question the
  ; display path now depends on, so it does not sit behind the one
  ; deliberate crash in this program.
  DgGroupG()
  DgGroupF()

  PutS("=== ")
  PrintDec((dgChecks - dgFails) - dgIncon)
  PutS(" of ")
  PrintDec(dgChecks)
  PutS(" cases agree with the CPU, ")
  PrintDec(dgFails)
  PutS(" failed, ")
  PrintDec(dgIncon)
  PutS(" inconclusive ===")
  PutNl()

  ; INCONCLUSIVE IS REPORTED AHEAD OF THE VERDICT AND IT IS NOT A PASS.
  ; A group G negative control that failed to reproduce its hazard has
  ; taken its positive twin down with it, and a summary that folded that
  ; into "everything agreed" would be asserting the one thing this run
  ; did not establish.
  If dgIncon > 0
    PutS("!! ")
    PrintDec(dgIncon)
    PutS(" coherency negative control(s) did not reproduce their hazard, so the")
    PutNl()
    PutS("   matching positive cases prove nothing. THE CACHE DISCIPLINE IS")
    PutNl()
    PutS("   UNPROVEN ON THIS RUN whatever the failure count says. First thing to")
    PutNl()
    PutS("   check is the MMU line at the top: with the caches off there is no")
    PutNl()
    PutS("   hazard to provoke and every group G control comes out like this.")
    PutNl()
  EndIf

  If dgFails = 0
    PutS("Every rectangle the engine drew is byte-identical to the one the CPU")
    PutNl()
    PutS("drew, across the whole 64 KiB and not merely inside the rectangle.")
    PutNl()
  Else
    PutS("Read the group a failure came from before reading the failure.")
    PutNl()
    PutS("  A or C  a real defect in the control block this file builds.")
    PutNl()
    PutS("  A2 row ladder   the fill row limit is REAL. Put the largest width that")
    PutNl()
    PutS("                  passed into #DMA_FILL_MAX_ROW_BYTES in dma.pi4; the")
    PutNl()
    PutS("                  refusal is already wired in behind it.")
    PutNl()
    PutS("  A2 stride ladder  the STRIDE word is mispacked, or D_STRIDE needs a")
    PutNl()
    PutS("                  tighter cap than the field's own 32767.")
    PutNl()
    PutS("  B       an alignment constraint to document, not a defect.")
    PutNl()
    PutS("  G1/G4/G6  a POSITIVE coherency case failed: the discipline as written")
    PutNl()
    PutS("            is not sufficient on this part. That is a defect in the")
    PutNl()
    PutS("            cache sequence, not in the transfer - the geometry groups")
    PutNl()
    PutS("            above will still be green while it is broken.")
    PutNl()
    PutS("  G2/G3/G5  cannot FAIL, only pass or come out inconclusive. See the")
    PutNl()
    PutS("            line above the verdict.")
    PutNl()
    PutS("  D       dma.pi4 must refuse overlap in BOTH directions.")
    PutNl()
    PutS("  F FAIL  a burst can still read past the 256-byte fill pattern - the")
    PutNl()
    PutS("          rung printed, ran and disagreed with the CPU.")
    PutNl()
    PutS("  F SILENT after a rung's announcement, the board hung on THAT rung. Set")
    PutNl()
    PutS("          #DMA_BURST_MAX_SAFE in dma.pi4 to one below it.")
    PutNl()
  EndIf
  UartDrain()

  If #DG_MMU = 1
    If MmuEnabled() = 1
      MmuDisable()
      PutS("MMU: off again - Anvil gets an untranslated machine back.")
      PutNl()
      UartDrain()
    EndIf
  EndIf

  ProcedureReturn dgFails
EndProcedure
